{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implementation note: brought the Bedrock spend-cap subsystem across as a single path checkout from origin/bedrock-daily-spend-cap onto a fresh origin/main worktree, rather than cherry-picking the branch commits, so the unsafe branch subject does not enter the review history.\n\nDrift plan decisions:\n- Keep aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.reviewbot_webhook_auth_rejections in HCL. The resource is declared by closed bug enrapt-capitalist-jackrabbit and is a real review-bot health monitor; removing it would hide a missing intended alarm. Satisfying that add requires applying the already-declared monitor outside this no-apply code task.\n- Do not reconcile aws_instance.gerrit in this change. The plan shows only a user_data hash update, and main.tf documents that this is an in-place, next-boot update on the production Gerrit host. Applying or rewriting it belongs in an operator-reviewed infrastructure apply, not in the spend-cap HCL restoration.\n\nRoot-cause prevention/detection: the corrected code path is to land the subsystem HCL through Gerrit before any production apply; terraform-drift remains the detector for state/HCL divergence and should go green only after the HCL lands and the separately missing alarm is handled by an operator apply."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789719892399074578,"uuid":"03c9ec0e-c636-4cd4-a93a-8a1ee42be07e"}